/*
 * Luna — every user-visible string, in one file.
 *
 * A key is  "<base>_<suffix>"  — the base is a descriptive name for the
 * screen and the specific text, never the English itself; the suffix is one
 * of  Luna  Core  Reader  Downloader  PriceTracker.  Each line reads:
 *
 *     "Onboarding_Util_headline_Luna" = "All your Luna apps.";
 *
 * At runtime, code calls  L10n.string("Onboarding_Util_headline")  — just
 * the base — and that resolves "<base>_<the running app's own suffix>" first
 * (Core for the central Luna app, else Reader/Downloader/PriceTracker), then
 * falls back to "<base>_Luna". So EVERY base key needs a "_Luna" line — it's
 * the fallback with nowhere further to go — and giving one app its own
 * wording is adding one more line, no Swift change at all:
 *
 *     "Onboarding_Util_headline_Reader" = "Read.";
 *
 * EDIT ONLY THE RIGHT-HAND SIDE for ordinary wording changes. The key is
 * fixed furniture: renaming it just detaches the line and the app falls back
 * to showing the raw key. Keep every  %@  %lld  %1$@  marker present and in
 * the same order across every suffix of one base key — they are where names,
 * counts and dates get substituted, and a value that drops or reorders one
 * relative to its siblings leaves an argument with nowhere to go.
 *
 * Keep the surrounding  "  =  "  ;  punctuation intact. A line that loses
 * its trailing  ;  takes the rest of its section down with it.
 *
 * Grouping and the comment above each group are HAND-AUTHORED: keys inside a
 * group are named so their alphabetical order matches the on-screen reading
 * order (numbered items get a zero-padded ordinal, e.g. _feature01/_feature02,
 * so "10" doesn't alphabetize before "2"). `scripts/strings_sync.py --write`
 * only appends placeholders for a referenced key with no entry yet and drops
 * lines no code references — it never re-sorts or re-groups, so a new key
 * belongs in whichever existing group it reads with, added by hand.
 */

